00:13:09,840 --> 00:13:10,000
Right?

214
00:13:10,160 --> 00:13:12,000
And so one, the bars in San Francisco are terrible.

215
00:13:12,480 --> 00:13:14,160
So running is actually a really good choice.

216
00:13:14,400 --> 00:13:16,880
But anyway, I mean, that materialized across the world.

217
00:13:17,040 --> 00:13:21,120
And so I was lucky enough to meet those two very early on in their journey.

218
00:13:21,600 --> 00:13:23,200
And so I was just a huge fan of the product.

219
00:13:23,680 --> 00:13:28,800
And so when they kind of gave us a call and said, Hey, we're thinking about doing a deal, you know, we kind of jumped right in.

220
00:13:29,360 --> 00:13:32,720
And so, you know, when you think about like why Strava bought Runna, right?

221
00:13:32,960 --> 00:13:34,240
Strava has a bunch of features on there.

222
00:13:34,400 --> 00:13:36,640
Technically, even had some old training features on there.

223
00:13:36,800 --> 00:13:38,800
So almost a competitive product, if you will.

224
00:13:39,360 --> 00:13:40,960
But I mean, I'll give you my view, right?

225
00:13:41,120 --> 00:13:43,200
And obviously, I don't sit within the Strava board.

226
00:13:43,360 --> 00:13:46,240
But this was really a one plus one equals six acquisition.

227
00:13:46,320 --> 00:13:48,320
And that, you know, people say one plus one equals three.

228
00:13:50,240 --> 00:13:53,600
And so what Strava is, Strava is an app for athletes, right?

229
00:13:53,760 --> 00:13:56,960
And so if you're not an athlete, Strava is not for you, right?

230
00:13:57,200 --> 00:13:59,920
And as you, a lot of people don't identify as athletes, right?

231
00:14:00,160 --> 00:14:02,400
And so a lot of people actually haven't heard of Strava, right?

232
00:14:02,560 --> 00:14:05,680
I'm from Ohio, and I can tell you most of my friends don't use Strava, right?

233
00:14:06,000 --> 00:14:07,440
San Francisco, everyone uses Strava.

234
00:14:07,680 --> 00:14:11,760
It's just a weird world that we live in out here in the seven by seven square miles of San Francisco.

235
00:14:12,240 --> 00:14:18,160
But what Run It did is they enabled people to go from being on the couch to running a 5k to running a 10k, and now they're an athlete.

236
00:14:18,560 --> 00:14:21,520
So for Strava, that's a massive TAM expansion, number one, right?

237
00:14:21,680 --> 00:14:26,720
So now all of a sudden you can actually be for people that are not athletes but want to be athletes but want to be an athlete, and that is a huge TAM.

238
00:14:27,040 --> 00:14:29,760
Two, Strava has done a great job with consumer subscription.

239
00:14:29,920 --> 00:14:31,200
It's one of the best apps out there.

240
00:14:31,360 --> 00:14:33,200
They've been around forever, great retention.

241
00:14:33,600 --> 00:14:35,280
Where they struggle though is pricing, right?

242
00:14:35,440 --> 00:14:39,920
They were unable, they have not built in a lot of different tiers to maximize price among consumers.

243
00:14:40,320 --> 00:14:44,640
And Runna actually adds a second tier that Strava can offer, which is a bundle, right?

244
00:14:44,800 --> 00:14:49,680
And we all can talk about bundling, unbundling, but in the consumer subscription, it's really popular with users.

245
00:14:50,080 --> 00:14:52,080
You already have a paid user who uses Strava.

246
00:14:52,400 --> 00:14:56,320
Runna integrated really well into Strava from the beginning, even before the acquisition, right?

247
00:14:56,560 --> 00:15:02,800
And so they were able to bring an additional ability to upsell a Strava runner or Strava consumer with another package.

248
00:15:02,960 --> 00:15:05,120
And so I think that's why it was a good deal.

249
00:15:05,440 --> 00:15:06,880
The run of founders didn't need to sell.

250
00:15:07,200 --> 00:15:12,000
You know, I don't think they had to do the deal, but Strava came calling and I think offered a pretty fair price for the business.

251
00:15:12,400 --> 00:15:23,920
Well, one of the things I was surprised at is that they didn't integrate immediately and that it is being run kind of as a separate business with this bundle, which I was surprised at.

252
00:15:24,160 --> 00:15:26,880
So any more color on the kind of masses?

253
00:15:27,520 --> 00:15:28,160
I'll give some color.

254
00:15:28,320 --> 00:15:30,320
And I mean, I don't know the Strava folks.

255
00:15:30,480 --> 00:15:31,520
I mean, we know the run.

256
00:15:31,920 --> 00:15:33,840
I know both teams, but not super well.

257
00:15:34,240 --> 00:15:41,040
But I think it would be kind of hard for what Eric was sayinging, it's like, you know, run is a come to a thing to like train for a thing, right?

258
00:15:41,280 --> 00:15:44,960
It's a very different flow versus like Strava is like Facebook for running, right?

259
00:15:45,200 --> 00:15:46,800
It's like you post all your stuff there.

260
00:15:46,960 --> 00:15:47,840
It's where you go to brag.

261
00:15:48,080 --> 00:15:49,200
It's your Instagram, right?

262
00:15:49,440 --> 00:15:52,160
And so it might take a while for them to fully integrate.

263
00:15:52,320 --> 00:15:53,200
That could be true as well.

264
00:15:53,600 --> 00:15:57,920
But I could also see a world where this is like a better approach, like where you keep them separate.

265
00:15:58,080 --> 00:15:59,680
And these are two very different properties.

266
00:15:59,920 --> 00:16:05,120
And, you know, you have a, I don't know if we're going to talk about it, but that you talk, you talk quite a bit about the conglomerates that are happening.

267
00:16:05,280 --> 00:16:17,840
And you can almost see, you know, maybe that's a strategy for Strava to kind of play in is where they become a pseudo, you know, a verticalized run roll up company of a few of these things and then they take advantage of all the benefits of cross selling and things like that.

268
00:16:18,000 --> 00:16:19,360
Yeah, I mean, I think it's a good question.

269
00:16:19,600 --> 00:16:21,360
Like, you know, these are two very good apps, right?

270
00:16:21,600 --> 00:16:23,200
And they're kind of they're both differently, right?

271
00:16:23,440 --> 00:16:26,000
So, and people just say, oh, just smash them together and call today.

272
00:16:26,160 --> 00:16:27,760
Well, like, that doesn't work, right?

273
00:16:28,080 --> 00:16:30,160
Consumers demand an excellent experience, right?

274
00:16:30,480 --> 00:16:31,840
And both businesses are doing really well.

275
00:16:32,000 --> 00:16:34,320
So if you try to smash them together and it works, it's wrong.

276
00:16:34,640 --> 00:16:36,560
You just wasted an entire acquisition, right?

277
00:16:36,880 --> 00:16:39,120
And I think both businesses can learn from each other, right?

278
00:16:39,360 --> 00:16:42,160
Run is amazing at using AI inside the app.

279
00:16:42,560 --> 00:16:43,360
Strava's a system of record.

280
00:16:43,600 --> 00:16:44,720
They don't, they don't have to do that yet.

281
00:16:44,880 --> 00:16:46,400
Now Mike and his team are doing some great stuff.

282
00:16:46,480 --> 00:16:47,680
So, like, keep your eyes peeled.

283
00:16:47,840 --> 00:16:54,320
And then also, so I think Strava's we're learning a lot from Runna on that front to, I mean, Runna does some really good performance marketing.

284
00:16:54,640 --> 00:16:55,200
Jacob's right.

285
00:16:55,440 --> 00:16:56,480
It's a little more episodic.

286
00:16:56,560 --> 00:16:57,920
Hey, I'm training for a marathon.

287
00:16:58,080 --> 00:17:00,240
Hey, I'm training for a big race, right?

288
00:17:00,480 --> 00:17:04,240
Versus Strava is more, hey, this is what I post my rides on every Tuesday, right?

289
00:17:04,480 --> 00:17:06,480
Those are two very different use cases for the consumers.

290
00:17:06,800 --> 00:17:10,800
And so I think that, uh, you know, I think that's right to keep them separate for now.

291
00:17:10,960 --> 00:17:26,880
And then I think they'll make a decision down the road when, when you integrate, if you integrate any advice for folks playing in these spaces where they could potentially be that Runna to Strava, where they could be that Tam expansion., the kind of soft intro.

292
00:17:27,520 --> 00:17:46,000
I feel like we're probably going to see more and more of these kinds of things happen over time where these giant apps that aren't necessarily great at aspects of the broader business that they're running, it could just be an opportunity for folks to build these kinds of adjunct apps and get bought.

293
00:17:46,480 --> 00:17:51,360
But like, what does it take to be that standalone product instead of Strava just building it in?

294
00:17:51,600 --> 00:17:53,440
Like, why didn't Strava just build it in?

295
00:17:53,600 --> 00:17:58,960
And how you become that kind of a product that not just gets ripped off by the big guys but gets bought by the big guys?

296
00:17:59,280 --> 00:18:00,640
Yeah, I mean, it's a great question.

297
00:18:00,960 --> 00:18:02,400
and it's really hard, right?

298
00:18:02,560 --> 00:18:05,920
Because tech even today, now it's easier to build stuff than it ever was.

299
00:18:06,480 --> 00:18:07,920
It's hard to build something really great.

300
00:18:08,480 --> 00:18:12,640
So what I always tell founders is if you're trying to build for an acquisition, that's hard.

301
00:18:12,880 --> 00:18:16,320
If you think about an acquisition, an acquisition is one company buying one company, right?

302
00:18:16,480 --> 00:18:22,560
So think about all the matching that has to occur for that, and it's really hard to build something that is exactly for one company, right?

303
00:18:22,800 --> 00:18:26,000
So what I tell founders is build a really great business that consumers love.

304
00:18:26,560 --> 00:18:28,000
That's your North Star, right?

305
00:18:28,240 --> 00:18:29,280
Everything else will work out.

306
00:18:29,840 --> 00:18:35,040
Let's just say you're running and maybe it wasn't going really well and it didn't work out with Strava, there would be another suitor.

307
00:18:35,360 --> 00:18:35,680
You know why?

308
00:18:36,080 --> 00:18:37,680
Because people love the app, right?

309
00:18:38,000 --> 00:18:45,200
And so my view is like just build something great, really get consumer love, and then the strategic corporate rationale side will work out, right?

310
00:18:45,360 --> 00:18:47,360
Because people want to be around products that people love.

311
00:18:47,600 --> 00:18:49,520
And so I think Strava saw that and made the move.

312
00:18:49,600 --> 00:18:57,920
And I think every other deal I've done with a strategic acquirer has that lens on it, which is the strategic wants access to consumers that love a product, period.

313
00:18:58,640 --> 00:18:58,800
Right?

314
00:18:58,960 --> 00:19:00,560
And so that's generally like guidance.

315
00:19:00,800 --> 00:19:00,880
Yeah.

316
00:19:01,040 --> 00:19:05,040
And to your earlier point, you know, Renta wasn't in a position where they had to sell.

317
00:19:05,600 --> 00:19:12,720
You know, they had a fantastic business and that's the most attractive acquisition and the best place to be in if you're negotiating one of these.

318
00:19:13,280 --> 00:19:15,200
acquisitions is like, we have a great business.

319
00:19:15,600 --> 00:19:16,080
It's growing.

320
00:19:16,800 --> 00:19:17,280
People love it.

321
00:19:17,920 --> 00:19:22,160
Much better position to be in than needing a sale to happen to make the business work.

322
00:19:22,480 --> 00:19:23,280
Yeah, exactly.

323
00:19:23,360 --> 00:19:24,800
Yeah, you don't want to be in that spot ever.

324
00:19:25,280 --> 00:19:32,400
Early in the report, you had a page titled CSS State of the Union, and in that you talk about a lot about AI.

325
00:19:32,560 --> 00:19:34,800
It almost felt like the whole page was devoted to AI.

326
00:19:35,280 --> 00:19:42,320
And it's kind of the 900 pound meta gorilla in the industry right now of like, what's going on with AI?

327
00:19:42,640 --> 00:19:43,760
How much of it's a headwind?

328
00:19:43,920 --> 00:19:45,280
How much of it's a tailwind?

329
00:19:45,920 --> 00:19:48,480
And you started with the headwinds, and I'd like to start there.

330
00:19:49,440 --> 00:19:56,320
What do you see as headwinds consumer subscription founders should be thinking about in this age of AI?

331
00:19:56,880 --> 00:20:01,120
Yeah, I mean, so this is the first year we've ever done this State of the Union letter.

332
00:20:01,440 --> 00:20:09,600
And we did it kind of because I was just feeling, man, I'm getting so many questions that are incredibly nuanced, putting together a slide with a couple of bowls just won't do it.

333
00:20:09,760 --> 00:20:09,920
Right.

334
00:20:10,000 --> 00:20:16,800
And so I was like, okay, how do I write, like long form, right, what I think are impacting my clients and future clients today?

335
00:20:17,840 --> 00:20:19,280
And then just think it through, right?

336
00:20:19,520 --> 00:20:23,840
And like the answer we're getting from investors every day is, is AI a headwind or tailwind here?

337
00:20:24,000 --> 00:20:26,400
And the answer is it's a shitty answer, but it depends.

338
00:20:27,280 --> 00:20:29,120
And it depends on a bunch of factors.

339
00:20:29,360 --> 00:20:33,280
And so, you know, we kind of went through and just said, like, here's where we think the headwinds are today, right?

340
00:20:33,600 --> 00:20:36,560
And I expect that to change materially over the next couple of years.

341
00:20:36,720 --> 00:20:38,720
And then here's where we think the tailwinds are today.

342
00:20:39,200 --> 00:20:41,360
And we expect that to change materially over the next couple of years.

343
00:20:41,520 --> 00:20:42,480
And just try to list it out.

344
00:20:42,960 --> 00:20:45,200
And so, yeah, we went to the negatives first, right?

345
00:20:45,360 --> 00:20:48,080
Because I think you gotta start, you want to start with the bad news first.

346
00:20:48,720 --> 00:20:59,840
And so the big thing we've discovered, and I'm sure you guys are experiencing this firsthand, is Google, once the number one source of traffic to the internet, is now no longer the sole default, right?

347
00:21:00,000 --> 00:21:08,080
So if you're a builder or a marketer, right, and then your job is to get your product in front of someone, SEO, your website, your content, that was it.

348
00:21:08,320 --> 00:21:08,960
That was what you did.

349
00:21:09,200 --> 00:21:15,520
You know, the whole thing you did was optimized to make sure the Google crawler found your site and then surface that up, right?

350
00:21:15,680 --> 00:21:25,360
And if it wasn't doing it organically through SEO, you man, you're putting money into Google for SEM, for search engine marketing, and making sure you showed up as one of the ten blue links if you weren't organically there, right?

351
00:21:25,520 --> 00:21:27,680
And that applied to business that are around for 30 years.

352
00:21:27,840 --> 00:21:29,760
It applies for business that are around for 30 days.

353
00:21:29,840 --> 00:21:31,360
You ran that exact same playbook.

354
00:21:31,840 --> 00:21:34,320
And that fractured a lot over the last two years.

355
00:21:34,480 --> 00:21:43,760
And I think it's going to fracture further where people are starting their discovery or their recommendation to a problem they have with one of their AI tools, pick out whatever you want.

356
00:21:44,400 --> 00:21:52,480
And that is the first time and probably since Google was created and maybe five years after Google came to be the powerhouse where access to the internet fractured.

357
00:21:53,200 --> 00:21:59,920
And so like it does require a new skill set for entrepreneurs and marketers to make sure their products get in front of people where they can't rely on Google.

358
00:22:00,640 --> 00:22:07,200
And so I've had clients, I've had buddies that are running businesses where their SEO is dropping thirty, forty percent year over year.

359
00:22:07,520 --> 00:22:18,400
You've seen a bunch of articles come out with a lot of the major I'll call legacy publishers that just published articles on the internet, Wikipedia for sure, where they're seeing traffic drops, material traffic drops that will change the business model.

360
00:22:18,880 --> 00:22:20,400
So that's absolutely a headwind, right?

361
00:22:20,640 --> 00:22:22,000
So I think that's a big, big deal.

362
00:22:22,080 --> 00:22:28,960
And we kind of compared it with the shift from desktop to mobile, where you have to really do everything, right?

363
00:22:29,280 --> 00:22:32,240
And at the same time, you can't afford to let SEO break.

364
00:22:32,640 --> 00:22:39,280
So you can't just optimize for ChatGPT and put a bunch of bullets on your website because Google still will represent the majority of your traffic.

365
00:22:39,680 --> 00:22:43,120
The question is, does it represent the majority of your high intent traffic?

366
00:22:43,600 --> 00:22:49,200
And that is what the jury is still out is if AI is delivering high intent traffic with click throughs, right?

367
00:22:49,360 --> 00:22:55,600
And a lot of people also, strangely enough, they'll search on ChatGPT and then go search Google for the answer that ChatGPT gave them, right?

368
00:22:55,840 --> 00:22:59,920
So you're actually seeing two different attribution is like one of the hardest problems.

369
00:23:00,000 --> 00:23:03,680
You're seeing two different attribution issues with that specifically.

370
00:23:04,000 --> 00:23:10,640
So I think people are probably actually undercounting how much ChatGPT or Perplexity or whatever tool people are using is contributing to traffic these days.

371
00:23:11,200 --> 00:23:16,560
One of the headwinds you didn't list was that AI is going to consume all apps.

372
00:23:16,880 --> 00:23:23,360
And I didn't watch the whole thing, but I saw a clip of Elon Musk saying that within five years there wouldn't be apps.

373
00:23:23,840 --> 00:23:28,240
Everything would just be AI and the AI would just magically deliver everything you ever wanted.

374
00:23:29,120 --> 00:23:30,320
I don't think that's going to happen.

375
00:23:31,040 --> 00:23:36,400
Any thoughts on, I'm sure this is a question that you pose to yourself and have had people pose to you.

376
00:23:37,520 --> 00:23:45,680
What do you see as those real headwinds over the next three to five years of AI kind of consuming more and more use cases?

377
00:23:45,920 --> 00:23:48,000
Yeah, I think the answer is it's definitely going to do more.

378
00:23:48,480 --> 00:23:49,360
Definitely going to do more.

379
00:23:49,440 --> 00:23:56,720
There's no doubt in my mind that whatever you're using with ChatGPT or your AI tool today, it will be more in two to three years, for sure.

380
00:23:57,200 --> 00:23:57,600
Guaranteed.

381
00:23:58,240 --> 00:23:59,520
And so then the question is, right?

382
00:24:00,000 --> 00:24:06,880
Like what we've heard this, the same kind of fear, I'll call it fear mongering or FUD, like fear, uncertainty, doubt about the consumer ecosystem for years.

383
00:24:07,040 --> 00:24:12,080
And I think the last time we heard it was in 2010, 2011, Apple, Google, Facebook, they're just going to build everything.

384
00:24:12,240 --> 00:24:12,400
Right.

385
00:24:12,560 --> 00:24:13,680
No point making anything else.

386
00:24:13,840 --> 00:24:14,400
Guess what, guys?

387
00:24:14,640 --> 00:24:15,040
Game's over.

388
00:24:15,280 --> 00:24:15,520
Get out.

389
00:24:16,000 --> 00:24:17,200
And I think we're seeing the same thing.

390
00:24:18,000 --> 00:24:23,280
That was the number one, like, VC objection for like half a decade, which was like, what if Google does it?

391
00:24:23,440 --> 00:24:24,320
Google's going to do it.

392
00:24:24,720 --> 00:24:24,880
Right.

393
00:24:25,200 --> 00:24:26,720
I mean, hell, they told that to Facebook.

394
00:24:27,120 --> 00:24:27,600
It's so funny.

395
00:24:27,840 --> 00:24:28,480
You don't hear that ever.

396
00:24:28,800 --> 00:24:29,760
Google's just going to build Facebook.

397
00:24:30,000 --> 00:24:30,880
Don't worry about building Facebook.

398
00:24:31,200 --> 00:24:32,000
Google's just going to do it.

399
00:24:32,240 --> 00:24:32,320
Yeah.

400
00:24:32,560 --> 00:24:32,720
Right.

401
00:24:32,960 --> 00:24:35,040
They probably will build something that's useful.

402
00:24:35,280 --> 00:24:37,280
But I mean, that's the beauty of of consumer, right?

403
00:24:37,440 --> 00:24:47,680
It, you know, there's definitely the big winners, but you can easily build a great business that does 10 million in revenue and pays you 3 million in cash a year and be a competitor, and your Facebook could be a competitor for you, for sure, right?

404
00:24:48,080 --> 00:24:51,200
And so, you know, you can get weather for free on your iPhone, but it's terrible.

405
00:24:51,600 --> 00:24:54,000
Or you can pay someone and get a lot better information.

406
00:24:54,240 --> 00:25:02,080
So I think this is kind of like if I'm a founder and we talk about this in our report later, like you definitely have to have a game plan to compete against AI, right?

407
00:25:02,240 --> 00:25:07,040
I don't think you can say, great, we're just going to do the same thing we've been doing for the last five years and hope to win, right?

408
00:25:07,280 --> 00:25:11,680
Because AI will come after you and they'll come after you to buy licensing data from you or your competitor.

409
00:25:12,400 --> 00:25:18,080
They will start building more and more functionality in there that people will just start there and maybe never end up with you.

410
00:25:18,400 --> 00:25:20,880
And I think that's, yeah, that's kind of the competitive threat.

411
00:25:21,280 --> 00:25:29,200
We talked about it a little bit, David, but I think the page right after our opening line was like, hey, we don't think AI is going to take out the consumer ecosystem at all.

412
00:25:29,440 --> 00:25:31,280
I think it'll take some for sure.

413
00:25:31,440 --> 00:25:32,720
I definitely think they'll be losers.

414
00:25:33,120 --> 00:25:41,040
This might be a silly question because we're so early on the exponential curve, but like, has that, can you all think of a single app AI has replaced for you?

415
00:25:41,760 --> 00:25:44,480
Like maybe I could say Google, but I still Google stuff, right?

416
00:25:44,640 --> 00:25:45,360
It kind of just depends.

417
00:25:45,600 --> 00:25:47,840
Google's AI results are so good too, right?

418
00:25:48,000 --> 00:25:48,000
